        {
          "key": "WindowManager::GetWindowAtPoint",
          "kind": "method",
          "doc": {
            "summary": "Get this application's topmost window at a screen point",
            "details": "Hit-tests the window stack from front to back and returns the first visible window that contains `point`. If the frontmost window there belongs to another application, the point is considered covered and nullptr is returned. This is the query a drag-and-drop or tear-off gesture needs to find the window under the cursor, typically with the window being dragged excluded.\n\n```cpp\nauto session_window = session.GetWindowId();\nauto target = WindowManager::GetInstance().GetWindowAtPoint(cursor, session_window);\nif (target && target->GetId() == main_window_id) {\n    // highlight the drop zone\n}\n```",
            "params": [
              {
                "name": "point",
                "text": "Screen coordinates, as returned by Window::GetPosition()."
              },
              {
                "name": "excluded_window_id",
                "text": "A window to look through, such as the window being dragged; pass 0 to exclude nothing."
              }
            ],
            "returns": "The window, or nullptr if no window of this application is frontmost at `point`.",
            "notes": [
              {
                "kind": "note",
                "text": "On Wayland other applications' windows are not visible to the process, and the stacking order of this application's own windows is approximated with the focused window first."
              }
            ]
          },
